  "raw_response": "def solve(grid):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a grid containing a single non\u2011zero rectangular (usually square) region,\n    swap the colors of the border and the interior of that region.\n\n    The border is defined as all cells of the region that lie on its outermost\n    perimeter. All remaining cells of the region form the interior.\n    The algorithm:\n    1. Find the bounding box of all non\u2011zero cells.\n    2. Determine the color that appears on the border (outer color).\n    3. Determine the color(s) that appear inside but not on the border.\n       If there are multiple, pick the most frequent one (the \u201cinner\u201d color).\n    4. Produce a new grid of the same size as the bounding box where\n       border cells are filled with the inner color and interior cells are\n       filled with the outer color.\n    5. Return this cropped grid.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Find bounding box of non\u2011zero cells\n    rows = len(grid)\n    cols = len(grid[0]) if rows else 0\n    min_r = rows\n    max_r = -1\n    min_c = cols\n    max_c = -1\n    for r in range(rows):\n        for c in range(cols):\n            if grid[r][c] != 0:\n                if r < min_r:\n                    min_r = r\n                if r > max_r:\n                    max_r = r\n                if c < min_c:\n                    min_c = c\n                if c > max_c:\n                    max_c = c\n\n    # If there is no non\u2011zero cell, return empty list (should not happen in task)\n    if max_r < min_r or max_c < min_c:\n        return []\n\n    height = max_r - min_r + 1\n    width = max_c - min_c + 1\n\n    # Determine outer (border) color\n    outer_color = None\n    for r in (min_r, max_r):\n        for c in range(min_c, max_c + 1):\n            if grid[r][c] != 0:\n                outer_color = grid[r][c]\n                break\n        if outer_color is not None:\n            break\n    if outer_color is None:\n        for c in (min_c, max_c):\n            for r in range(min_r, max_r + 1):\n                if grid[r][c] != 0:\n                    outer_color = grid[r][c]\n                    break\n            if outer_color is not None:\n                break\n\n    # Gather interior colors (excluding border)\n    interior_colors = {}\n    for r in range(min_r + 1, max_r):\n        for c in range(min_c + 1, max_c):\n            val = grid[r][c]\n            if val != 0:\n                interior_colors[val] = interior_colors.get(val, 0) + 1\n\n    # If no distinct inner color exists, just keep the original region unchanged\n    if not interior_colors or outer_color not in interior_colors:\n        # return the original region as\u2011is\n        return [[grid[min_r + i][min_c + j] for j in range(width)] for i in range(height)]\n\n    # Choose the most frequent interior color that is different from the border\n    inner_color = max(\n        (cnt, color) for color, cnt in interior_colors.items() if color != outer_color\n    )[1]\n\n    # Build the transformed region\n    result = []\n    for r in range(min_r, max_r + 1):\n        row = []\n        for c in range(min_c, max_c + 1):\n            if r == min_r or r == max_r or c == min_c or c == max_c:\n                # border cell -> inner color\n                row.append(inner_color)\n            else:\n                # interior cell -> outer color\n                row.append(outer_color)\n        result.append(row)\n\n    return result",
